Wait, actually, perhaps the problem can be rephrased in terms of graph coloring. If we consider each friend as a node, and an edge between two friends if they are wholesome (i.e., their combined collection has all n stickers). Then the problem is to color the edges in such a way that no two edges connected to the same node are... Wait, maybe not. Actually, the problem is to divide the friends into tables so that on each table, no two friends are wholesome. Which would mean that for any two friends on the same table, they are not wholesome. 

Therefore, each table is a subset of the friends such that no two friends in the subset are wholesome. Therefore, we need to find the minimal number of subsets (tables) such that each subset has the property that no two friends in the subset are wholesome. 

But how can we model this? Well, if we can find that for any two friends in the same table, their combined set is missing at least one sticker. Therefore, each table must be a set of friends where every pair has a common missing sticker. So, for example, if two friends have a combined set missing one sticker, then they are not wholesome. 

Alternatively, if we consider the complement, for each friend, the set of stickers they are missing from their collection. Let me think. 

Suppose each friend's collection is a subset of the n stickers, and we can represent their set as a subset of {1, 2, ..., n}. For two friends, if their union of collections is the entire set {1, 2, ..., n}, then their combination is a full set. Otherwise, they are not. 

